      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we can use the trigonometric identity for the cosine of a sum:\n\\[\n\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B\n\\]\nSetting \\(A = 2x\\) and \\(B = 3x\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x\n\\]\nGiven that \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we can substitute this into the equation:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\cos 2x \\cos 3x = 0\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0\n\\]\nThe general solution for \\(\\cos 5x = 0\\) is:\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ (2k + 1) \\quad \\text{for integer } k\n\\]\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ (2k + 1)\n\\]\nThe smallest positive angle occurs when \\(k = 0\\):\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ\n\\]\nThus, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}\n\\]",
