    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "From the given equation $\\sin(2x)\\sin(3x)=\\cos(2x)\\cos(3x)$, we start by expressing it in terms of product-to-sum identity. We have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(2x)\\sin(3x) = -\\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(2 - 3x) - \\cos(2 + 3x)]\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cos(2x)\\cos(3x) = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(2 - 3x) + \\cos(2 + 3x)]\n\\]\n\nExpanding these, we get:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(2 - 3x) - \\cos(2 + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(2 - 3x) + \\cos(2 + 3x)]\n\\]\n\nSimplifying further:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{1}{2}(\\cos(2 - 3x) - \\cos(2 + 3x)) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos(2 - 3x) + \\cos(2 + 3x))\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 2:\n\n\\[\n-\\cos(2 - 3x) + \\cos(2 + 3x) = \\cos(2 - 3x) + \\cos(2 + 3x)\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives:\n\n\\[\n-2\\cos(2 - 3x) = 2\\cos(2 + 3x)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying even more:\n\n\\[\n-\\cos(2 - 3x) = \\cos(2 + 3x)\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(5x) = \\sin(2x)\n\\]\n\nGiven $\\sin A = \\sin B \\Rightarrow A = B + 360^\\circ n$ or $A = 180^\\circ - B + 360^\\circ n$ for integer $n$, we consider two cases:\n\n1) When $A = B + 360^\\circ n$:\n\n\\[\n5x = 2x + 360^\\circ n \\implies 3x = 360^\\circ n \\implies x = 120^\\circ n\n\\]\n\nGiven $0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ$, we have $x = x$, which is always true but not relevant after simplification.\n\n2) When $A = 180^\\circ - B + 360^\\circ n$:\n\n\\[\n5x = 180^\\circ - 2x + 360^\\circ n \\implies 7x = 180^\\circ + 360^\\circ n \\implies x = \\frac{180^\\circ + 360^\\circ n}{7}\n\\]\n\nFor $0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ$:\n\n\\[\n0^\\circ < \\frac{180^\\circ + 360^\\circ n}{7} < 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSolving:\n\n\\[\n0^\\circ < 180^\\circ + 360^\\circ n < 1260^\\circ \\implies -180^\\circ < 360^\\circ n < 1080^\\circ \\implies -\\frac{1}{2} < n < \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nHowever, $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, which means $n = 0$ or $-1$.\n\nConsidering only the positive value:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180^\\circ}{7} \\approx 25.71^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive angle $x$ satisfying the condition is $\\boxed{25.71^\\circ}$.",
