      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x.\n\\]\nWe can use the identity for the product of sines and cosines:\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B)] \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) + \\cos (A + B)].\n\\]\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the angle subtraction formulas directly. We can rewrite the given equation as:\n\\[\n\\cos(3x - 2x) = \\cos(5x).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\cos x = \\cos 5x.\n\\]\nThe general solution to the equation $\\cos A = \\cos B$ is given by:\n\\[\nA = B + 360^\\circ k \\quad \\text{or} \\quad A = -B + 360^\\circ k,\n\\]\nwhere $k$ is an integer. Applying this to our equation, we get:\n\\[\nx = 5x + 360^\\circ k \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -5x + 360^\\circ k.\n\\]\nSolving the first equation:\n\\[\nx - 5x = 360^\\circ k \\implies -4x = 360^\\circ k \\implies x = -90^\\circ k.\n\\]\nSince we are looking for the smallest positive angle, we take $k = -1$, giving us $x = 90^\\circ$.\n\nSolving the second equation:\n\\[\nx + 5x = 360^\\circ k \\implies 6x = 360^\\circ k \\implies x = 60^\\circ k.\n\\]\nThe smallest positive value for $x$ is $60^\\circ$.\n\nThus, the smallest positive angle $x$ that satisfies the given equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{60^\\circ}.\n\\]",
